/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE TUBE COUNT, SOLVED AGAINST THE HOT SPOT.

  The plant's feed is fixed (0/feed), so the number of tubes sets the mass
  velocity G in each one, and G sets three things at once: the time a
  parcel of gas spends on the catalyst (more tubes -> more catalyst -> more
  conversion), the pressure drop (more tubes -> slower gas -> a smaller
  drop -> higher partial pressures), and the bed-to-wall coefficient,
  which Li & Finlayson correlate as G^0.8 (more tubes -> SLOWER gas -> a
  WORSE wall).  All three push the same way, so here MORE tubes run
  HOTTER -- measured, 2026-10-12, on this case:

        tubes      2500    3000    3500    4000    5000
        T_max [K]  641.7   648.4   656.8   668.5   747.4  (runaway)
        X(oX)      0.30    0.46    0.62    0.76    0.98

  and the design question is the largest count -- the most catalyst, the
  most conversion -- at which the hot spot stays at its limit.  The count
  is a real number here (a Newton manipulates it); the sizer rounds it UP
  to whole tubes and says so.  The limit, 665 K (392 C), is AUTHOR-SET.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

type designSpec;

manipulate
(
    { variable nTubes;   initial 3000;  min 2200;  max 6000; }   // tubes
);

targets
(
    { path reactor.T_max;   value 665.0;   tol 0.01; }   // K -- the hot-spot limit, AUTHOR-SET
);

options
{
    maxIter      40;
    tolF         1.0;
    fdStep       1.0e-3;
}

report
{
    file     designspec_history.csv;
}
